#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:70
msgid "Frequently asked questions (FAQ)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:73
msgid "Do I need to use Elasticsearch or AWS OpenSearch?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:75
msgid ""
"No. Enterprise search engines are designed for large Enterprise "
"deployments to run highly efficient database searches in a cluster "
"environment. The default Mattermost database search starts to show "
"performance degradation at around 2.5 million posts, depending on the "
"specifications for the database server. If you expect your Mattermost "
"server to have more than 2.5 million posts, we recommend using "
"Elasticsearch or AWS OpenSearch for optimum search performance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:78
msgid ""
"Should I install Elasticsearch or OpenSearch on the same machine as "
"Mattermost Server?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:80
msgid ""
"No. We strongly recommend that you install Elasticsearch or AWS "
"OpenSearch on a dedicated machine that's separate from the Mattermost "
"server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:83
msgid "What types of indexes are created?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:85
msgid ""
"Mattermost creates three types of indexes: users, channels, and posts. "
"Users and channels have one index each. Posts are aggregated by date, "
"into multiple indexes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:88
msgid "Can I pause an search indexing job?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:90
msgid ""
"Yes. From Mattermost v6.7, the search indexing job is resumable. Stopping"
" a server while the search indexing job is running puts the job in "
"pending status. The job resumes when the server restarts. System admins "
"can cancel an indexing job through the System Console."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:93
msgid "Can an index rollover policy be defined?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:95
msgid ""
"The :ref:`AggregatePostsAfterDays <administration-guide/configure"
"/environment-configuration-settings:aggregate search indexes>` "
"configuration setting defines a cutoff value. All posts preceding this "
"value are reindexed and aggregated into new and bigger indexes. The "
"default setting is 365 days."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:98
msgid "Are there any new search features offered with Elasticsearch?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:100
msgid ""
"The current implementation of Elasticsearch matches the search features "
"currently available with database search. The Mattermost team is working "
"on extending the Elasticsearch feature set with file name and content "
"search, date filters, and operators and modifiers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:103
msgid "Are my files stored in Elasticsearch or OpenSearch?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:105
msgid "No, files and attachments are not stored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:108
msgid "How do I monitor system health of an Elasticsearch server?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:110
msgid ""
"You can use this Prometheus exporter to monitor `various metrics "
"<https://github.com/justwatchcom/elasticsearch_exporter#metrics>`__ about"
" Elasticsearch: `justwatchcom/elasticsearch_exporter "
"<https://github.com/justwatchcom/elasticsearch_exporter>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:112
msgid ""
"You can also refer to this `article about Elasticsearch performance "
"monitoring <https://www.datadoghq.com/blog/monitor-elasticsearch-"
"performance-metrics/#key-elasticsearch-performance-metrics-to-"
"monitor>`__. It's not written specifically for Prometheus, which "
":doc:`Mattermost's performance monitoring </administration-guide/scale"
"/deploy-prometheus-grafana-for-performance-monitoring>` system uses, but "
"has several tips and best practices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:115
msgid "What form of data is sent to Elasticsearch or OpenSearch?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:117
msgid ""
"Mattermost communicates with Elasticsearch or OpenSearch through its REST"
" API using JSON messages for indexing and querying entities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:120
msgid "How much data is sent to Elasticsearch or OpenSearch and when?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:122
msgid ""
"Every time a message is published, a channel is created, or a user "
"changes, (either because their properties change e.g.: change of the "
"first name or because they join/leave a channel), the data associated "
"with that event is sent to Elasticsearch or OpenSearch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:124
msgid ""
"If search via Elasticsearch or OpenSearch is enabled, every search will "
"generate a query. If autocompletion is enabled, every user or channel "
"autocompletion associated with writing a message or user search will "
"generate a query."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:127
msgid "How do I know if a search job fails?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:129
msgid ""
"Mattermost provides the status of each Elasticsearch or OpenSearch "
"indexing job in **System Console > Environment > Elasticsearch**. Here "
"you can see if the job succeeded or failed, including the details of the "
"error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:131
msgid ""
"Failures are returned in the server logs. The error log begins with the "
"string ``Failed job`` and includes a job_id key/value pair. Search job "
"failures are identified with worker name "
"``EnterpriseElasticsearchAggregator`` and "
"``EnterpriseElasticsearchIndexer``. You can optionally create a script "
"that programmatically queries for such failures and notifies the "
"appropriate system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:134
msgid "How do I diagnose enterprise search connection issues?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:136
msgid ""
"From Mattermost v11, :doc:`Support Packet generation </administration-"
"guide/manage/admin/generating-support-packet>` automatically tests the "
"connection to your configured Elasticsearch or AWS OpenSearch server and "
"includes any connection errors in the Support Packet. This provides "
"valuable diagnostic information including server version, installed "
"plugins, and specific error details if the connection fails."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:138
msgid ""
"The enterprise search connection test results appear in the Support "
"Packet and can help identify configuration issues such as network "
"connectivity problems, authentication failures, or server availability "
"issues. If connection errors are present, they will be clearly documented"
" with specific error messages to aid in troubleshooting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:141
msgid "How does Mattermost handle Elasticsearch or OpenSearch outages?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:143
msgid ""
"From Mattermost v11.7, the server includes an automatic health monitor "
"for Elasticsearch and OpenSearch connections. The health monitor runs "
"periodic health checks and automatically manages the connection "
"lifecycle:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:145
msgid ""
"**Health checks**: The server checks the health of the search engine "
"cluster every 60 seconds. After 3 consecutive health check failures, the "
"engine is stopped and search falls back to the database."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:146
msgid ""
"**Fast-fail on first failure**: On the very first health check failure, "
"the engine is immediately marked as unhealthy and search requests are "
"routed to the database. This happens before the consecutive failure "
"threshold is reached, so users experience minimal disruption."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:147
msgid ""
"**Automatic retry**: When the search engine is unavailable, the server "
"retries connecting with exponential backoff, starting at 15 seconds and "
"doubling up to a maximum of 5 minutes between attempts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:148
msgid ""
"**Automatic recovery**: When the search engine becomes available again, "
"the server automatically reconnects and resumes using it for search "
"queries. No manual intervention or server restart is required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:149
msgid ""
"**Configuration changes**: Changes to Elasticsearch or OpenSearch "
"configuration settings, or license changes, immediately trigger the "
"health monitor to re-evaluate the connection state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/administration-guide/scale/enterprise-search.rst:150
msgid ""
"**Monitoring**: A ``mattermost_search_engine_status`` Prometheus metric "
"reports the health of the search engine (``1`` = healthy or not "
"configured, ``0`` = configured but unavailable). Use this metric to "
":doc:`set up alerts </administration-guide/scale/performance-alerting>` "
"for search engine outages. See :doc:`performance monitoring metrics "
"</administration-guide/scale/performance-monitoring-metrics>` for "
"details."
msgstr ""

